# Mobile store builds are on-demand

Merging to `staging` or `main` publishes **server images only**. It must **never** start an EAS
build or store submit for `apps/mobile`.

## Do

- Ship store binaries with local Make targets that call [`scripts/mobile/eas-store.sh`](scripts/mobile/eas-store.sh):
  `make mobile_eas_ios_build` / `mobile_eas_android_build`, then the matching `_submit` targets.
- Keep `EXPO_PUBLIC_*` and signing on EAS (profile → `environment` in [`apps/mobile/eas.json`](apps/mobile/eas.json)).
- When giving the operator a build or submit, list every prompt that command will ask and the
  exact answer, then give that one command. Give the next command only after those answers.
  Read [`scripts/mobile/eas-store.sh`](scripts/mobile/eas-store.sh) for the current prompts.
  One command per fenced block. A second command in the same paste is read as the answer to
  `read`, and a blank line is "no", which cancels the build. If a paste already queued the next
  command, answer the prompt on screen and do not give that command again. If that queued
  command is a submit for a platform whose build failed, tell them to press Ctrl+C. Submit
  takes the newest finished build for that platform, which may be an older binary.
- A store `build` refuses any uncommitted path. Commit or stash first, including edits to this
  rule made while a build was running. `submit` does not check the working tree.
- If a GitHub Actions path is added later, use **`workflow_dispatch` only**, behind an approval
  Environment that holds `EXPO_TOKEN`. No `push:` / `pull_request:` triggers for `eas build` or
  `eas submit`.

## Prompt answers

The script asks first, then `eas` may ask more. Answers that stay the same:

| Prompt | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| `Start an EAS … cloud build …? [y/N]` | `y` |
| `iOS app only uses standard/exempt encryption? (Y/n)` | `Y`. HTTPS only. Skipped once `ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption: false` is in `app.config.ts` `infoPlist`. EAS cannot write that into the dynamic config; it prints the snippet instead. |
| `Would you like to install the "expo-updates" package and configure EAS Update now?` | `n`. The `beta` and `production` profiles set a `channel`, so EAS asks this on every build. This app does not use EAS Update. |
| `Do you want to log in to your Apple account?` | `n` when the line above says it is using remote iOS credentials. Those credentials are already on EAS. Logging in lets EAS generate new ones. |
| Generate a new iOS certificate, profile, or Android keystore | No. Reuse the credentials already on EAS. A new keystore does not match Play's upload key, and a new iOS profile must still be `com.podverse.app.next`. |
| `Upload versionCode N to Play as a draft? [y/N]` | `y` only when the `Build:` line above it is the build just made (same commit and versionCode). A queued submit after a failed build prints an older finished build. Press Return, which is no, and cancels. |
| `Type the versionCode (N) to roll it out:` | The printed versionCode, exactly |
| `Type the build number (N) to submit to TestFlight:` | The printed build number, exactly |

`eas build --non-interactive` does not answer these. It exits when one is unset, so the script does not pass it. The script's own `[y/N]` stays.

## Don't

- Do not add or restore workflows that run `eas build` / `eas submit` on push to `develop`,
  `staging`, or `main`.
- Do not couple mobile store jobs into [`publish-staging.yml`](.github/workflows/publish-staging.yml)
  or [`publish-main.yml`](.github/workflows/publish-main.yml) with `needs:`.
- Do not treat the mobile `npm ci` step in server publish validate as a store release — it exists
  only so lint can type-check `apps/mobile`.
- Do not point a store build at the old production listing. Identity stays
  `com.podverse.app.next` until the operator explicitly replaces that app. See
  **mobile-next-store-identity**.
- Do not ship over-the-air updates. This app does not use EAS Update. JavaScript changes go out
  in the next store binary, the same as native changes.

## Over-the-air updates

Do not install `expo-updates`, add an `updates` config, run `eas update`, or publish to an EAS
Update channel.

Older notes that say EAS Update is for JavaScript-only fixes are not a request to turn that on.

If the operator asks for over-the-air updates, stop and confirm in that conversation that they
want it. It is a major change. Do not start it from an implication, a plan, or an older doc.

